@Tutorial(time: 20) {
    @Intro(title: "Character animation") {
        In this final lesson, we'll use Godot's built-in animation tools to make
        our characters float and flap. You'll learn to design animations in the
        editor and use code to make your game feel alive.

        @Image(source: squash-the-creeps-final.gif)

        We'll start with an introduction to using the animation editor.
    }
    @Section(title: "Using the animation editor") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            The engine comes with tools to author animations in the editor.
            You can then use the code to play and control them at runtime.

            It features a toolbar and the animation drop-down menu at the
            top, a track editor in the middle that's currently empty, and
            filter, snap, and zoom options at the bottom.
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Open the player scene, select the `Player` node, and add an
                [AnimationPlayer](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fanimationplayer.html#class-animationplayer)
                node.

                The _Animation_ dock appears in the bottom panel.

                @Image(source: animation_player_dock.webp)
            }
            @Step {
                Let's create an animation. Tap on _Animation -> New_.

                @Image(source: 02.new_animation.webp)
            }
            @Step {
                Name the animation "float".

                @Image(source: 03.float_name.webp)
            }
            @Step {
                Once you've created the animation, the timeline appears
                with numbers representing time in seconds.

                We want the animation to start playback automatically at
                the start of the game. Also, it should loop.

                To do so, you can tap the autoplay button
                (![Autoplay](autoplay_button.webp)) in the animation
                toolbar and the looping arrows, respectively.

                @Image(source: 04.autoplay_and_loop.webp)
            }
            @Step {
                You can also pin the animation editor by tapping the pin
                icon in the top-right. This prevents it from folding when
                you tap on the viewport and deselect the nodes.

                @Image(source: 05.pin_icon.webp)
            }
            @Step {
                Set the animation duration to `1.2` seconds in the
                top-right of the dock.

                You should see the gray ribbon widen a bit. It shows you
                the start and end of your animation and the vertical blue
                line is your time cursor.

                You can tap and drag the slider in the bottom-right to
                zoom in and out of the timeline.

                @Image(source: 06.animation_duration.webp)
            }
        }
    }
    @Section(title: "The float animation") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            With the animation player node, you can animate most properties
            on as many nodes as you need. Notice the key icon next to
            properties in the _Inspector_. You can tap any of them to
            create a keyframe, a time and value pair for the corresponding
            property. The keyframe gets inserted where your time cursor is
            in the timeline.

            Let's insert our first keys. Here, we will animate both the
            position and the rotation of the `Character` node.

            > Note: Animations update the properties of the animated
            > nodes every frame, overriding initial values. If we directly
            > animated the _Player_ node, it would prevent us from moving
            > it in code. This is where the _Pivot_ node comes in handy:
            > even though we animated the _Character_, we can still move
            > and rotate the _Pivot_ and layer changes on top of the
            > animation in a script.

            > Note: A lecture on the principles of animation is beyond the
            > scope of this tutorial. Just note that you don't want to
            > time and space everything evenly. Instead, animators play
            > with timing and spacing, two core animation principles. You
            > want to offset and contrast in your character's motion to
            > make them feel alive.
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Select the `Character` and in the _Inspector_ expand the
                _Transform_ section. Tap the key icon next to _Position_,
                and _Rotation_.

                For this tutorial, just create RESET Track(s) which is
                the default choice.

                Two tracks appear in the editor with a diamond icon
                representing each keyframe.

                @Image(source: 09.creating_first_keyframe.webp)
            }
            @Step {
                You can tap and drag on the diamonds to move them in time.
                Move the position key to `0.3` seconds and the rotation
                key to `0.1` seconds.

                @Image(source: 11.moving_keys.webp)
            }
            @Step {
                Move the time cursor to `0.5` seconds by tapping and
                dragging on the gray timeline, or by entering it into the
                input field.

                In the _Inspector_, set the _Position_'s _Y_ axis to
                `0.65` meters and the _Rotation_' _X_ axis to `8`.

                If you don't see the properties in the _Inspector_ panel,
                first tap on the `Character` node again in the _Scene_
                dock.

                Create a keyframe for both properties.

                @Image(source: 12.second_keys_values.webp)
            }
            @Step {
                Now, move the position keyframe to `0.7` seconds by
                dragging it on the timeline.

                @Image(source: 13.second_keys.webp)
            }
            @Step {
                Move the time cursor to the end of the animation, at
                `1.2` seconds. Set the Y position to about `0.35` and the
                X rotation to `-9` degrees. Once again, create a key for
                both properties.

                @Image(source: animation_final_keyframes.webp)
            }
            @Step {
                You can preview the result by tapping the play button or
                pressing Shift + D. Tap the stop button or press S to
                stop playback.

                @Image(source: 14.play_button.webp)
            }
            You can see that the engine interpolates between your
            keyframes to produce a continuous animation. At the moment,
            though, the motion feels very robotic. This is because the
            default interpolation is linear, causing constant
            transitions, unlike how living things move in the real
            world.

            We can control the transition between keyframes using easing
            curves.

            @Step {
                Tap and drag around the first two keys in the timeline to
                box select them.

                You can edit the properties of both keys simultaneously
                in the _Inspector_, where you can see an _Easing_
                property.

                @Image(source: 15.box_select.webp)
            }
            @Step {
                Tap and drag on the curve, pulling it towards the left.
                This will make it ease-out, that is to say, transition
                fast initially and slow down as the time cursor reaches
                the next keyframe.

                Play the animation again to see the difference. The first
                half should already feel a bit bouncier.

                @Image(source: 17.ease_out.webp)
            }
            @Step {
                Apply an ease-out to the second keyframe in the rotation
                track.

                @Image(source: 18.ease_out_second_rotation_key.webp)
            }
            @Step {
                Do the opposite for the second position keyframe, dragging
                it to the right.

                Your animation should look something like this.

                @Image(source: 20.float_animation.gif)
            }
            If you play the game, the player's creature will now float!

            If the creature is a little too close to the floor, you can
            move the `Pivot` up to offset it.
        }
    }
    @Section(title: "Controlling the animation in code") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            We can use code to control the animation playback based on the
            player's input. Let's change the animation speed when the
            character is moving.

            This code makes it so when the player moves, we multiply the
            playback speed by `4`. When they stop, we reset it to normal.

            We mentioned that the `Pivot` could layer transforms on top of
            the animation. We can make the character arc when jumping using
            the following line of code. Add it at the end of
            `_physics_process()`.
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Open the `Player`'s script by tapping the script icon next
                to it.

                @Image(source: 21.script_icon.webp)
            }
            @Step {
                In `_physics_process()`, after the line where we check
                the `direction` vector, add the code that bumps the
                animation `speed_scale` when the player is moving and
                resets it to `1` when they stop.

                @Code(name: "player.gd", file: player-10.gd, previousFile: player-09.gd)
            }
            And with that, you finished coding your first complete 3D
            game.

            **Congratulations**!

            In the next part, we'll quickly recap what you learned and
            give you some links to keep learning more. But for now, here
            are the complete `player.gd` and `mob.gd` so you can check
            your code against them.

            @Step {
                Here's the _Player_ script.

                @Code(name: "player.gd", file: player-10.gd)
            }
            @Step {
                And the _Mob_'s script.

                @Code(name: "mob.gd", file: mob-05.gd)
            }
        }
    }
    @Section(title: "Animating the mobs") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Here's another nice trick with animations in Godot: as long as
            you use a similar node structure, you can copy them to
            different scenes.

            For example, both the `Mob` and the `Player` scenes have a
            `Pivot` and a `Character` node, so we can reuse animations
            between them.

            We can change the playback speed based on the creature's
            `random_speed`.
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Open the _Player_ scene, select the AnimationPlayer node
                and then tap on **Animation > Manage Animations...**. Tap
                the _Copy animation to clipboard_ button (two small
                squares) alongside the _float_ animation. Tap OK to close
                the window.

                @Image(source: fix-me.png, alt: "The Manage Animations dialog with the Copy button highlighted")
            }
            @Step {
                Then open `mob.tscn`, create an
                [AnimationPlayer](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fanimationplayer.html#class-animationplayer)
                child node and select it. Tap **Animation > Manage
                Animations**, then **New Library**. You should see the
                message "Global library will be created." Leave the text
                field blank and tap OK. Tap the _Paste_ icon (clipboard)
                and it should appear in the window. Tap OK to close the
                window.

                @Image(source: fix-me.png, alt: "Pasting the float animation into the Mob scene")
            }
            @Step {
                Next, make sure that the autoplay button
                (![Autoplay](autoplay_button.webp)) and the looping
                arrows (Animation looping) are also turned on in the
                animation editor in the bottom panel. That's it; all
                monsters will now play the float animation.

                @Image(source: fix-me.png, alt: "The autoplay and looping arrows enabled in the animation editor")
            }
            @Step {
                Open the _Mob_'s script and at the end of the
                `initialize()` function, add the line that scales the
                animation speed by `random_speed / min_speed`.

                @Code(name: "mob.gd", file: mob-06.gd, previousFile: mob-05.gd)
            }
        }
    }
}
